## DESCRIPTION
## intervals of increasing and decreasing; first derivative
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by jjh2b 8/22/06

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Increasing/decreasing functions and local extrema)
## Date(8/23/07)
## Institution(Union College)
## Author(K. Lesh)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus: Early Transcendentals')
## AuthorText1('Rogawski')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('4.3')
## Problem1('27')
## KEYWORDS('calculus','increasing','decreasing')

DOCUMENT();        # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
	'PGstandard.pl',
	'PGML.pl',
	'PGcourse.pl' 
);

$a = random(2,9,2);
$b = non_zero_random(-9,9,2);

$f=Formula("$a x^3 + $b")->reduce;


$inc=List(Interval(   "(-infinity,infinity)"   ));
$dec=List(   'NONE'    );
$xrelamaxs=List(   'NONE'    );
$xrelamins=List(  'NONE'    );

BEGIN_PGML
Let [` f(x) = [$f] `].  Find the open intervals on which [` f `] is increasing (decreasing).  Then, determine the [` x`]-coordinates of all relative maxima (minima).

a) [`f`] is increasing on the interval: [_]{$inc}
b) [`f`] is decreasing on the interval: [_]{$dec}
c) The relative maxima of [`f`] occur at [`x=\;`] [_]{$xrelamaxs}
d) The relative minima of [`f`] occur at [`x=\;`] [_]{$xrelamaxs}

[@ helpLink('intervals') @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Find the critical point where [`f'(x) = 0`] or [`f'(x)`] is undefined.


 Then, substitute an x-value from each interval back to [` f'(x) `] to decide if the function is increasing or decreasing.
END_PGML_HINT
ENDDOCUMENT();